[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 2, "caption": "Figure 1. From sequential reading to selective genomic scanning. (a) Sparse, discontinuous genomic signals make sequential modeling inefficient. (b) OCR-inspired genomic modeling enables efficient, reconstructible visual compression. (c) 2D CNNs outperform 1D CNNs in accuracy-efficiency trade-offs on eQTL prediction. (d) Vision tokens substantially reduce the effective token count.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure about genomic sequence modeling and compression.\n\nPanel (a) contrasts natural language with genomic sequence structure. It shows a transformer attention mechanism over words in a sentence, labeled as “dense, continuous & compositional,” compared with a genomic coordinate track labeled “sparse, discontinuous & jump-like semantics.” The genomic diagram includes regulatory region, exons, introns, long-range interaction, chromosome coordinates, and a low-information background across an ultra-long genomic sequence.\n\nPanel (b) presents an OCR-inspired genomic modeling pipeline. DNA documents are split into pages and patches, then processed into optical DNA. It frames DNA as a document rather than a sentence and lists goals such as visual-style selective scanning, reconstructible compression, and efficient ultra-long sequence handling. The transformation shown is genomic sequence to document/page/patch-style representations.\n\nPanel (c) is a scatter plot titled “Accuracy vs Training Efficiency,” plotting average AUROC on 8 tasks against epoch time in seconds, comparing models including ResNet, TinyNet, MobileNetV3, CNN/1D-CNN variants, HyenaDNA, e-OTL prediction, and 2D-CNN. It indicates tradeoffs between parameter count, accuracy, and training speed.\n\nPanel (d) compares tokenization/compression schemes: base-level tokens with no compression, k-mer tokens with about 3x compression, and “Vision Tokens (Ours)” with about 20 base tokens per patch and about 20x compression. It visually shows DNA bases grouped into larger tokens/patches.\n\nVisible finding/theme: genomic sequences have sparse long-range semantics, so compression for efficient modeling requires structure-aware or “semantic” understanding rather than simple dense token processing."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Figure 2. Overview of OpticalDNA. (a) Render a 1D genomic sequence into a multi-page DNA document with bounding-box annotations. (b) Construct six OCR-style prompted genomic tasks. (c) Pretrain a visual encoder-document decoder under prompt supervision.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel schematic describing a DNA document generation, visual encoding, OCR-style pretraining, and decoding pipeline.\n\nPanel (a), “DNA document generation and visual encoding,” shows a 1D DNA sequence beginning “ACGGTT…” transformed into a multi-page DNA document with spatial annotations. A highlighted sequence region is connected to a bounding-box annotation with fields including sequence text and coordinate-like values. The document is patchified into `p x n x 16 x 16` patches, then passed through a frozen visual encoder labeled “SAM-Conv → CLIP-L / 399.8M,” producing `p x (n/16) x d` visual tokens.\n\nPanel (b), “OCR-style prompt pretraining tasks,” lists six genomic reasoning primitives: T1 Reading, T2 Grounding, T3 ROI, T4 Completion, T5 Retrieval, and T6 Recognition. Each task is illustrated with small DNA document snippets and prompt examples such as “Read the sequence,” “Locate each line in the sequence,” “Read sequence within region,” “Complete masked-region,” “Locate ATG,” and “Classify document.” The tasks are aligned with capabilities labeled Perception, Localization, Scan, Inference, Query, and Global understanding.\n\nPanel (c), “Trainable projection, fusion, and decoding,” shows the visual tokens entering a trainable projector labeled 2.6M parameters, then a trainable multi-page fusion module labeled 6.6M, producing `(n/16) x d` visual tokens. These are passed into a trainable document decoder labeled “DeepSeek-3B MOE-A570M,” conditioned by a task instruction interface with “Prompt” and “Response.” The output is trained with a loss labeled `L_pt`.\n\nBiological source object: DNA/genome sequence text represented as document-like pages with genomic regions and coordinate annotations. The figure presents a model architecture for converting DNA sequences into visual document representations and training OCR-like tasks for genomic reasoning."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 7, "caption": "Figure 3. Ablation on DNALONGBENCH (AUROC). Green arrows and numbers denote the relative gain over DeepSeek-OCR.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a grouped bar chart comparing two methods, `Deepseek-OCR` and `OpticalDNA`, across categories labeled `CCF`, `WB`, `Thyroid`, `SNSES`, `SSELL`, `MS`, `NT`, `AT`, `AS`, and `Average`.\n\nVisible elements:\n- One chart panel with a y-axis ranging approximately from `0.700` to `0.950`.\n- Blue bars represent `Deepseek-OCR`.\n- Green bars represent `OpticalDNA`.\n- Each category has paired bars.\n- Green percentage annotations with upward arrows appear above categories, indicating relative improvement for `OpticalDNA`: `0.55%`, `0.54%`, `16.86%`, `14.30%`, `2.77%`, `7.09%`, `3.14%`, `1.38%`, `3.37%`, and `5.37%`.\n- A legend at the bottom maps colors to the two methods.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, transformations, or model interface diagrams are visible. The finding shown is that `OpticalDNA` has higher values than `Deepseek-OCR` in all displayed categories, with the largest visible improvements for `Thyroid` and `SNSES`, and an average improvement of `5.37%`."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Figure 4. Grad-CAM visualization on multi-page fusion for a donor case (two pages). Purple boxes indicate donor splice sites; numbers denote page-level mean attribution.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows two side-by-side text panels containing dense nucleotide/DNA-like sequences highlighted in green. Each panel appears to be a page or sequence block comparison view.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Left panel labeled `Page#0: 0.530`\n- Right panel labeled `Page#4: 0.585`\n- A horizontal color/score bar at the bottom ranging from `0` to `1`, with midpoint `0.5`\n- Most sequence text is shaded green, suggesting a continuous heatmap or confidence/similarity overlay\n- A vertical divider separates the two panels\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA/RNA nucleotide strings composed primarily of A, C, G, and T characters\n\nLikely transformation/interface:\n- Sequence text has been rendered as page images and overlaid with a green intensity map, possibly representing similarity, attribution, relevance, or matching score.\n- The page-level numeric labels may indicate scores for each extracted page/panel.\n\nVisible finding:\n- Both panels contain strong green-highlighted sequence regions, with the right panel having a slightly higher displayed score (`0.585`) than the left (`0.530`)."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 5. Rendering robustness on the Adipose Subcutaneous eQTL task. Bars are grouped by perturbation type (Appendix N for details) and the dashed line marks the original layout performance.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a horizontal bar chart comparing AUROC values on an “Oligopso Subcutaneous eQTL” task.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Single panel bar chart with grouped feature/ablation categories.\n- X-axis: “AUROC on Oligopso Subcutaneous eQTL”, spanning roughly 0.750 to 0.875.\n- Y-axis labels include: Original, Dense, Sparse, Wide, Tall, Wide+Dense, Tall+Sparse, Win-32, Win-48, Win-64, Block-64, Shift-TL, Shift-Inward, Sans, Serif.\n- A vertical dashed reference line marks “Original 0.813”.\n- Each bar has a numeric AUROC label.\n- Legend categories:\n  - Original, gray\n  - Text Density, blue\n  - Aspect Ratio / Page Shape, green\n  - Fixed-Window / Fragment Arrangement, red\n  - Starting Position, orange\n  - Font, purple\n\nFindings shown:\n- Original baseline AUROC is 0.813.\n- Highest visible score is Wide+Dense at 0.858.\n- Dense scores 0.838; Sparse scores 0.819.\n- Font variants score highly: Sans 0.839 and Serif 0.835.\n- Window/block variants are lower, around 0.758 to 0.777.\n- Shift-Inward nearly matches baseline at 0.811, while Shift-TL is lower at 0.783.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- No biological images, cells, tissues, molecules, anatomical structures, or microscopy objects are visible.\n- The biological context is only textual: eQTL prediction for an “Oligopso Subcutaneous” target."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 6. Low-resolution robustness on the Adipose Subcutaneous eQTL task. AUROC remains stable under moderate downsampling and degrades mainly at extreme resolutions.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle line chart showing model performance across image downsampling resolutions.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: “Downsampled resolution” with labels `64×64`, `128×128`, `256×256`, `320×320`, `640×640`.\n- Y-axis: “AUROC”, ranging roughly from `0.76` to `0.84`.\n- Green line with circular markers connects AUROC values:\n  - `64×64`: `0.768`\n  - `128×128`: `0.799`\n  - `256×256`: `0.812`\n  - `320×320`: `0.828`\n  - `640×640`: `0.813`\n- Dashed horizontal reference line labeled “Native 640×640” near AUROC `0.812`.\n- Finding: AUROC improves from low resolution to `320×320`, peaks at `0.828`, then decreases slightly at `640×640` to `0.813`, close to the native-resolution reference line.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, transformations, or model interface diagrams are visible in this extracted image."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 7. AUROC of AS eQTL under different image resolutions.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a bar chart comparing model performance across four DNA dataset categories:\n\n- X-axis labels:\n  - Caduceus (HG38)\n  - JanusDNA (HG38)\n  - OpticalDNA (HG38)\n  - OpticalDNA (Rice)\n\n- Y-axis label: Score, ranging roughly from 0.4 to 0.8.\n\n- Metrics shown:\n  - Accuracy: solid gray line with circular markers.\n  - AUROC: dashed gray line with circular markers.\n\n- Bars appear color-coded by method/source:\n  - Blue bars for Caduceus and JanusDNA on HG38.\n  - Green bars for OpticalDNA on HG38 and Rice.\n\nVisible numeric values:\n- Caduceus (HG38): Accuracy 0.449, AUROC 0.568.\n- JanusDNA (HG38): Accuracy 0.477, AUROC 0.673.\n- OpticalDNA (HG38): Accuracy 0.595, AUROC 0.745.\n- OpticalDNA (Rice): Accuracy 0.598, AUROC 0.741.\n\nBiological source objects: genomic DNA datasets labeled HG38, corresponding to the human reference genome, and Rice.\n\nFindings shown: OpticalDNA has higher Accuracy and AUROC than Caduceus and JanusDNA on HG38, and OpticalDNA shows similar performance on HG38 and Rice."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 22, "caption": "Table S9. Unified response template for Task T2 (DNA Transcription with Spatial Grounding).", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a cropped document/table snippet labeled “Response Template” with placeholder text such as `<ref>SEQUENCE</ref>` and `<det>[[img-id,x1,y1,x2,y2]]</det>`. It contains no scientific panels, biological objects, model diagrams, experimental data, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 23, "caption": "Table S11. Unified response template for Task T3 (ROI-Based DNA Transcription).", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThis is a screenshot of a response template containing placeholder markup (`<ref>SEQUENCE</ref><det>[[img_id,x1,y1,x2,y2]]</det>`). It has no scientific panels, biological objects, model outputs, experimental data, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 24, "caption": "Table S13. Unified